# Build context for agents/agent-history/Dockerfile, agents/document-pipeline/Dockerfile, agents/audio-snippets/Dockerfile, agents/advocate/Dockerfile, agents/reorder-agent/Dockerfile, agents/order-desk/Dockerfile, agents/prior-authorization-agent/Dockerfile, agents/insurer-intake-agent/Dockerfile, agents/bid-desk/Dockerfile, agents/sourcing-agent/Dockerfile, services/mock-systems/Dockerfile and scripts/operator/Dockerfile is the repo root.
# node_modules is the entry that matters. A copy installed on the host carries host-specific pnpm links, and COPY would lay it over
# the image's own install. The image installs its own from the lockfile.
# Env files are ignored at any depth: a key left in a .env anywhere in the tree must never reach the build context.
# The model files a developer fetched into agents/audio-snippets/.models (about 150 MB) stay out too: the image fetches its own at build time.
# Every folder named test stays out of every image. A Dockerfile copies a whole package or agent folder, and the image runs the sources and not the tests.
node_modules
**/node_modules
**/.models
**/test
**/__pycache__
.git
.github
.superpowers
**/.env
**/.env.*
*.log
dist
build
docs
specs
deploy
# The scripts folder stays out, except the operator pack and the checks that it reuses. The last line that matches a path decides, and a line above a re-include does not reach into the folder that the
# re-include lets in. So the lines after the re-includes leave out of those two folders what the lines above leave out of the others (test folders, env files and node_modules), and two more files that
# can hold a secret: the key files of the pack, which end in .seed, and .npmrc.
scripts/*
!scripts/operator
!scripts/checks
scripts/**/test
scripts/**/.env
scripts/**/.env.*
scripts/**/node_modules
scripts/**/*.seed
scripts/**/.npmrc
